      "url": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2605.03143",
      "pdf_url": "http://arxiv.org/pdf/2605.03143",
      "category": "via-citation",
      "published": "",
      "collected": "2026-06-04",
      "abstract": "Recent advances in large language models have led to the rise of software systems (i.e. agents) that execute with increasing autonomy on behalf of users in open, multi-party settings, interacting with untrusted counterparts and managing private information. Choreographic programming offers correct-by-construction protocol-design for such settings, but assumes cooperative participants -- it has no notion of agent self-interest, that is, why an agent will follow a protocol. In this talk we introduce Pact, a choreographic language extended with operations to describe agent choices and preferences, drawing from the rich literature of game theory. Every Pact protocol maps to a formal game, allowing protocol designers to reason about game-theoretic properties of their protocols, such as solving for decision policies. We present Pact's design and a preliminary implementation -- a bounded-rational solver that computes decision policies over Pact protocols -- and findings from applying this language to multi-party coordination with self-interested agentic participants.",
      "abstract_zh": "近期大语言模型进展推动了开放多场景下代表用户自主运行、与不可信方交互并管理隐私信息的智能体系统兴起。编排编程可为这类场景提供构造正确的协议设计，但假设参与者合作，未考虑智能体自利性。本文引入结合博弈论、扩展了描述智能体选择与偏好操作的编排语言Pact，每个Pact协议可映射为形式化博弈，支撑协议性质推理，还给出了设计、初步实现与应用发现。",
